  UnixPermissionTool.strings        — per-surface P1/S10 i18n table (every web t() key + native chrome).

Parity reproduced exactly from the web source (cross-platform value parity, not a "corrected" version):
  symbolic = (octal.length === 3 && /^[0-7]{3}$/.test(octal)) ? PERMS[d0]+PERMS[d1]+PERMS[d2] : null ;
  PERMS = {7:rwx,6:rw-,5:r-x,4:r--,3:-wx,2:-w-,1:--x,0:---} ; owner/group/other = symbolic 0..3 / 3..6 / 6.. ;
  presets (source order) 755/644/700/600/777/444 with `octal (symbolic)` labels.

i18n keys extracted from the web source (table "UnixPermissionTool"): "Unix Perm", "Unix Perm Desc",
"Octal Perm", "Presets", "Owner", "Group", "Other". The web en.json carries Presets/Owner/Group/Other
verbatim and has NO entry for Unix Perm / Unix Perm Desc / Octal Perm, so i18next renders those keys
as-is — the native fallbacks reproduce that exactly (parity). Native-only chrome adds the invalid-input
hint + the symbolic a11y label. The view holds no bare English literals (all via UnixPermissionToolStrings).

States: this is a pure synchronous compute tool — the web source has NO data fetching (no useQuery), so
loading / error / stale / offline are N/A by parity (no async source exists to be in those states).
The web source's two real branches are reproduced and tested: valid octal → content breakdown; invalid
octal → the breakdown is hidden (native renders a friendly hint, never a blank box).

